      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A hardline populist approach to managing a national economic crisis is rooted in the idea that the globalist elite's pursuit of profit has led to a catastrophic erosion of national sovereignty, as multinational corporations and international institutions prioritize their interests over those of the nation-state. This erosion is seen as a betrayal of the social contract, as the ruling elite has abandoned its duty to protect the common good and instead serves the interests of a globalized elite. Populists argue that the nation-state's primary function is to safeguard the welfare of its citizens, and that this can only be achieved through the protection of domestic industries, jobs, and cultural identity through measures such as tariffs, quotas, and strict immigration controls. By prioritizing national sovereignty, populists believe that the nation-state can reclaim its ability to make decisions that benefit its citizens, rather than being beholden to the whims of global markets and institutions.",
      "source": "target",
